<p>Before you hire a logo designer, ask how they'll learn about your business, what exactly is included, how many revisions you get, which files you'll receive at the end and who owns the finished logo. Those five answers tell you most of what you need to know, and they're the ones that cause the most trouble when nobody asks.</p>

<p>The other questions below fill in the gaps: timelines, how the logo will be tested, and what happens if you need changes a year from now. You can use this list with any designer, studio or agency in Albuquerque or anywhere else. The goal is simple: no surprises after you've paid.</p>

<h2>1. How will you learn about my business before you start designing?</h2>

<p>A logo is a business decision, not just a drawing. A designer who jumps straight to sketches without asking about your customers, competitors and goals is guessing. Listen for questions about who you serve, what makes you different, where the logo will be used and where you want the business to be in five years.</p>

<p>At DYAD, every project starts with a BRAND$TORM™ consultation, a collaborative discovery session about purpose, long term vision and marketing strategy. Whoever you hire, expect some version of that conversation.</p>

<h2>2. Can I see logos you've made for businesses like mine?</h2>

<p>A portfolio shows you taste and skill. It's even better if you can see a logo in use: on a sign, a shirt, a truck or a website, not just floating on a white background. Ask about one or two projects in detail. What was the problem? What did the client ask for? What changed along the way?</p>

<p>Reviews help too. Read them on Google and on the designer's site, and notice what people praise. Fast and friendly is nice. "The logo works everywhere we use it" is better.</p>

<h2>3. What exactly is included, and is it in writing?</h2>

<p>"A logo" can mean very different things. One designer delivers a single file. Another delivers a full set of layouts, color versions, a simple style guide and business card artwork. Neither is wrong, but you need to know which one you're paying for.</p>

<p>Ask for a written proposal or contract that lists the deliverables before work begins. That's how we handle it at DYAD: exact deliverables are spelled out in the proposal so both sides agree on the finish line before anyone starts drawing.</p>

<h2>4. How many concepts and revision rounds do I get?</h2>

<p>Find out how many initial directions you'll see, how many rounds of changes are included and what counts as a round. Also ask what happens if you want more. Some designers charge for extra rounds, some include them, and some cap them to keep the project moving. Any of those can be fair as long as it's clear up front.</p>

<p>One tip: collect feedback from your partners or key staff before each round and send it as one list. Scattered notes from five people are how a two round project turns into six.</p>

<h2>5. Which logo files will I receive?</h2>

<p>This is the question most people forget, and the one print shops and embroiderers will make you wish you'd asked. You want vector files (AI, EPS, SVG or a vector PDF) for print, signs and embroidery, plus PNG files with transparent backgrounds for your website and social media.</p>

<p>Ask for every layout (for example, horizontal, stacked and icon only) in full color, one color, black and white. Our <a href="/blog/logo-file-checklist-print-embroidery">logo file checklist</a> breaks down what each file does and what to send to which vendor, including how a Rusty Nail Saloon logo was rebuilt by hand so it could actually be embroidered.</p>

<h2>6. Who owns the logo and the copyright when we're done?</h2>

<p>Don't assume that paying for a logo means you own it. Under U.S. law, a freelancer or outside studio generally owns the copyright in what they create unless there's a written agreement saying otherwise. The U.S. Copyright Office explains the rules in its <a href="https://www.copyright.gov/circs/circ30.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">guide to works made for hire</a>.</p>

<p>Ask for a contract that transfers the copyright in the final logo to you once the project is paid in full. It's also fair to ask whether the designer can show the work in their portfolio. Most will want to, and most clients are happy to say yes. This isn't legal advice, so if your brand is a big asset, have an attorney look over the agreement.</p>

<h2>7. Is the design original, and have you checked it?</h2>

<p>Ask whether the logo will be drawn from scratch or built from templates, stock icons or AI generated images. Template and stock marks can show up on other businesses' signs, and they can be hard or impossible to protect. Ask the designer how they make sure the final mark doesn't look too close to an existing brand in your industry.</p>

<p>If you plan to register a trademark, you can do a first check yourself with the <a href="https://www.uspto.gov/trademarks/search/federal-trademark-searching" target="_blank" rel="noopener">USPTO's federal trademark search</a>. A trademark attorney can do a proper clearance search if you need one.</p>

<h2>8. How will you make sure the logo works in real life?</h2>

<p>A logo has to work at the size of a favicon and the size of a building sign, in one color on a cap, and in white on a dark photo. Ask how the designer tests that. Good answers sound like: "We'll show it small and large, on a mockup of your sign, in one color and reversed out."</p>

<p>This is also where cheap and AI generated logos tend to fall apart. A busy mark with thin lines and soft gradients can look great on a screen and turn into mush when it's stitched or shrunk. If you're unsure whether to keep and clean up what you already have, our post on <a href="/blog/rebrand-or-refresh-logo-albuquerque">rebranding vs. refreshing a logo</a> can help you decide.</p>

<h2>9. What's the timeline, and what do you need from me?</h2>

<p>Ask for a realistic schedule from kickoff to final files, broken into steps. Then ask what you'll need to provide and how fast. Projects often stall on the client side: waiting on feedback, approvals from a partner or old files nobody can find. Knowing your part up front keeps things moving.</p>

<p>Also ask who you'll actually be working with. With a small studio it's often the same person from the first call to the final file. At a bigger agency you may be handed off to a team, which can work well if there's one clear point of contact.</p>

<h2>10. What happens after the logo is done?</h2>

<p>Your logo is the start, not the finish. Ask whether the designer offers a simple style guide, business cards, signage, website graphics or social media templates, and whether they're available for small edits later. Even if you don't need any of that now, you'll want to know who to call when the sign shop asks for a different version.</p>

<p>A short guide covering your colors, fonts and do's and don'ts keeps everything consistent as more people use your logo. We cover what goes in one in <a href="/blog/brand-style-guide-small-business">what a brand style guide should include</a>.</p>

<h2>Red flags to watch for</h2>

<ul>
  <li>No questions about your business before showing designs.</li>
  <li>No written proposal, or one that just says "logo design."</li>
  <li>Only JPG or PNG files offered, with no vector file.</li>
  <li>Vague or missing answers about who owns the final logo.</li>
  <li>Portfolio pieces that look like templates you've seen elsewhere.</li>
  <li>Pressure to decide fast, or a price that seems too good to be true.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What a good result feels like</h2>

<p>When the process works, the logo doesn't just look good in a presentation. Your team gets behind it. After Gorman Industries rolled out its refined logo, the company put it this way:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>"The rollout of the new logo to our employees went really well. Everyone loves it!!"</p>
  <cite>Gorman Industries, Albuquerque, NM</cite>
</blockquote>

<p>If you're comparing designers right now, feel free to bring this list to your first call with us too. You can see how we approach <a href="/branding-logo-design">branding and logo design in Albuquerque</a>, and we'll happily answer all ten questions before you commit to anything.</p>
